A prop read off a shape's `#id` is answered at build time with the expression
the prop was given.

The id hoists like any other now -- a family change is no reason to refuse one
-- and what differs is the read. `passNodePropReads` asks the tree: a primitive
claims `#[gen.renders(identity)]` when a `#id` on what it draws names something
still there while the program runs. Every widget primitive does. The four
`draw` primitives do not, because `passShapeDraw` splices a shape into the
calls that paint it and leaves nothing to ask.

So `dot.r` becomes 5.0 and folds into the string: `<span>r is 5</span>`, a
constant in the markup, with nothing left that names the shape.

The widget half of the claim -- that the same read off a `ui.text` handle is
left alone -- is not here, and the reason is a defect this does not fix. No
target can actually emit a prop read off a node handle. On the Go mutation
platforms `GoIRContext.Select` ends at `operand + "." + ExportName(field)`, so
`label.value` is `m.label.Value` against a `widget.Label` that spells it
`Text`, which does not compile; on html the read reaches the emitter and comes
out as an empty span that nothing ever fills. Both predate this pass. What the
pass changed is that it now *declines* to answer those, because html's
`element` and the widget primitives claim `#[gen.renders(identity)]` -- so a
fixture pinning the widget half would be pinning the defect.

`error_tree_family_id_barrier.sngl` was the program here, refusing the id
outright.

deny out/none/html/index.html `\bdot\b` -- the shape is gone by codegen; naming it emits a reference to nothing, which is what this pass ended

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import draw "sngl:ui/draw"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

ui.window #w(title="shapes") {
    draw.canvas(width=100px, height=100px) {
        draw.circle #dot(cx=10.0, cy=10.0, r=5.0)
    }

    ui.text(value="r is {dot.r}")
}
